#version 450
// Issue #439 ladder, arm (d) — THE UNPACK ARM. Added after issue #440's RGP capture.
//
// GEOMETRICALLY IDENTICAL to arm (a): 16x16 tile, one wave64 subgroup, BK=32, same LDS padding,
// same staging, same store. The ONLY difference is FAST_UNPACK, which makes the PQ2_0 dequant
// cheaper in ALU without touching the tile. So (a) -> (d) isolates unpack cost exactly the way
// (a) -> (b) -> (c) isolates arithmetic intensity.
//
// It exists because the tile hypothesis and the hardware counters disagree. #440 measured, on the
// shipping kernel at lm_head: WMMA 0.46% of issue slots (the matrix pipe 99.5% IDLE), VALU 43.9%,
// LDS 14.8%, SALU 12.5%, waits 14.5% — about 95 VALU ops per WMMA, a ratio set by the dequant
// rather than by the tile. On that reading, enlarging the tile schedules an idle pipe better and
// should buy little. The #439 ladder measured (a) -> (c) at 3.89x on lm_head, which it plainly
// did not. This arm attacks the constraint the counters name, so the two can be compared on the
// same shape in the same session.
//
// What FAST_UNPACK changes (see the block in matmul_pq2_0_f32_gemm_ladder.glsl):
//   * the staged value is {-scale, +/-0, +scale}, so it is chosen, not computed — two compares
//     and two selects per element instead of int-extract + int->float + f32 multiply + f32->f16;
//   * the group scale covers 128 elements = 4 BK=32 chunks, so it is refreshed once per group
//     instead of once per chunk.
//
// Deliberately NOT a full unpack rewrite: no wide uint loads, no bitfieldExtract batching, no
// relayout. One cheap arm, measured. The rewrite is its own issue.
#define BM 16
#define BN 16
#define WM 16
#define WN 16
#define NSG 1
#define WAVE 64
#define FAST_UNPACK

#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require
#include "matmul_pq2_0_f32_gemm_ladder.glsl"
